[REDIRECTIONS]

Redirections need to be applied individually ot each command, yet persist
inbetween certain commands (pipes, etc). We could try to handle all redirec-
tions in one place, that would mean we open fds in processes, that don't need
access to them though. The other option is to split it, and have the process,
that executes a whole line and creates child processes for a pipe create just
the pipe fds, which it provieds to the child processes (chidl process is given
a in and out redirection, if it isn't invalid, it is already set f.e.).

In any case the parent process needs to handle the creation of pipes. It needs
to store the read end of a created pipe for the next process and close opened
pipes as well as open new ones in proper timing if needed (command between
to pipes needs to be handed the read end of the previous pipe, open a new pipe
hand the child process the write end and kick of the child process. Only after
seperating from the child process the parent can close the previous pipe and
the new pipes write end.).

[SUBSHELL REDIRECTIONS]

In a line a subshell can be called with brackets. Subshells are executed in the
same way, that regular commands are, except, that they are also a group of
commands.

Subshells can be assigned redirections:

	"(cat -e || echo HELLO && echo WORLD) > out < in"

	In this case once the shell encounteres the subshell, it splits splits off
	into a chidl process that handles the subshell. This child process will
	handle initial redirections for the whole subshell, as well as potential
	pipe redirections.

[GROUPS AND COMMANDS]

Every group and command can have a redirection. Since groups and commands are
both accessible from the t_group struct it would make the most sense to store
the redirections there.

[Here Documents]
	
	From 'man bash'

   This type of redirection instructs the shell to read input from the current source un‐
   til  a  line  containing only delimiter (with no trailing blanks) is seen.  All of the
   lines read up to that point are then used as the standard input (or file descriptor  n
   if n is specified) for a command.

   The format of here-documents is:

		  [n]<<[-]word
				  here-document
		  delimiter

   No  parameter  and  variable expansion, command substitution, arithmetic expansion, or
   pathname expansion is performed on word.  If any part of word is quoted, the delimiter
   is the result of quote removal on word, and the lines in the here-document are not ex‐
   panded.  If word is unquoted, all lines of the here-document are subjected to  parame‐
   ter  expansion, command substitution, and arithmetic expansion, the character sequence
   \<newline> is ignored, and \ must be used to quote the characters \, $, and `.

   If the redirection operator is <<-, then all leading tab characters are stripped  from
   input  lines  and  the  line  containing delimiter.  This allows here-documents within
   shell scripts to be indented in a natural fashion.
